  "note": "Castilian Spanish. 27 letters: Ñ is a letter of the alphabet, CH and LL are not — the Academies removed them in 2010, so they are two letters each. Y is 'ye' in current usage; older speakers say 'i griega' and both are understood. 31-49 are written as three words, 'treinta y uno'. The accented vowels are not letters of the Spanish alphabet — they appear inside words and are never spawned in the Letters lesson, but a child collecting them inside a word still hears them named. Spanish calls the mark 'tilde', so 'a con tilde'; the two dots on Ü are the 'diéresis'.",
